    "data": {
      "A": "Investigate sorted box-position maintenance in move_me: skip ordering work on walking moves and restore order by shifting only the pushed box. Use A’s insertion-shift candidate as a hypothesis, not a proven win; preserve the sorted-box invariant used by hashing and equality, and benchmark against baseline.",
      "B": "Investigate box-collision checks in move_me. Since box positions are sorted, compare exact binary search for larger box counts with the existing linear scan for smaller counts. Check both the adjacent cell and push destination, retain exact collision handling, and measure whether the added search overhead pays off.",
      "C": "Do not make fine-grained OpenMP batching the default: its measured mean was slower. Explore only conservative, provably sound deadlock pruning beyond the existing live-cell check, retaining exhaustive BFS whenever a state is uncertain. Preserve shortest move count and required tie-breaking; consider parallel work only if measurements show a sufficiently large-frontier crossover and ordered merging remains correct.",
      "G": "Integrate one candidate at a time and measure the integrated program; do not assume gains add. A’s insertion-shift variant measured 0.0047162s versus the 0.0047517s baseline, but the difference is small. The hash guard (0.0048643s), B’s memmove variant (0.0049471s), and C’s OpenMP variant (0.0051177s) were slower in these runs. Recheck any candidate or combination before keeping it. Verify shortest-path and up/down/left/right tie-breaking behavior: the current move order and prepended frontier warrant an explicit audit.",
      "analysis": "The baseline was correct on 4 measured cases and averaged 0.0047517s. The best observed result, A’s insertion-shift change at 0.0047162s, is only about 0.7% faster; baseline samples ranged from 0.0046365s to 0.0049767s, so treat that as a hypothesis rather than a reliable win. The other measured candidates were slower. The official requirement is a shortest solution with up, down, left, right tie-breaking; optimizations must preserve complete state identity and BFS depth, and must not specialize to test cases or replace the search."
